![Old computer](cover.jpg)

What computer do we need in order to work as developers?.

There are many options on the market; in this short article we will show a few so you can choose according to your budget. We will present four options, from the cheapest to the most expensive, and detail their characteristics.

## Option 1: Retro (4 GB of RAM or less)

This is an option that can range from very cheap prices to very high prices. Everything will depend on how old the machine you want to get is and its availability on the market.

A cheap option can be equipment from the 2000s, like 2007 - 2010. They are well priced and can run many programming languages. You can find them at flea markets or they can even be given to you for free, since many people don't use them because (depending on the machine) they no longer work well with today's internet world.

For example, if you want to run Python on Windows 7 you can use it up to version 3.7. You can still run the latest version of Elixir and Erlang on a computer with Windows 7.

Although the recommendation is always to go for a Linux distribution like Debian or Fedora, which still support machines more than 10 years old with the latest security and software packages.

While these machines may not work well with current web requirements, they can be used as an experimentation lab for a huge diversity of languages. They are a great ally for learning to program, since what you learn using C, Pascal or another language can be put to use and will serve as a cornerstone.

Here is a list of programming languages that would run well on a machine with 4GB of ram or less, some would even work with less than 64 MB of ram.

- <https://www.freebasic.net/> (Basic)
- <https://www.lazarus-ide.org/> (Free Pascal)
- <https://gambas.sourceforge.net/> (Gambas Basic)
- <https://www.dosbox.com/> (DOS Emulator)
- <https://scratch.mit.edu/> (Scratch version 2)
- <https://smallbasic-publicwebsite.azurewebsites.net/> (MS Small Basic)

### FreeDOS

There is this free operating system <https://www.freedos.org/> that lets you run MSDOS programs. Which will give you access to the great classics of programming like Turbo Pascal or Turbo C.

- <https://archive.org/details/msdos_qbasic_megapack>
- <https://archive.org/details/msdos_borland_turbo_c_2.01>
- <https://archive.org/details/softwarelibrary_msdos_apps>

