### Legacy Versions of Programming Languages

Many programming languages have accompanied us for several decades, so if you wish you can use the version most compatible with the computer you acquired. Although using old versions can have some drawbacks, since languages evolve over time and if you learned on a legacy version and join a more recent project, you may need to re-learn many things.

Some languages you can use, but you'd have to be careful, since their modern versions can have many changes:

- Java
- Ruby
- Python
- Javascript
- PHP
- C#

If you will use this computer to learn, the best thing is always to go for the greats like *C*, *Assembler*, *BASIC* and *Pascal*, and perhaps some HTML and CSS wouldn't hurt.

### Success Case

![Dell Latitude e430](cover.jpg)

This is a computer from 2007. A Dell Latitude e430. It was found in a house during a cleanup and it was donated.

- Processor: Intel Centrino
- Ram: 2 GB DDR2 533 Mhz
- Disk: 128 GB CIF (Dead).

To repair it, a general cleanup and a change of thermal paste were done.

In addition, a new hard disk was bought, but since CIF hard disks are no longer manufactured, an adapter and an mSata disk were chosen.

- [CIF to mSata adapter](https://es.aliexpress.com/item/32826615019.html?spm=a2g0o.order_list.order_list_main.23.1aec194duL8JLU&gatewayAdapt=glo2esp)
- [256 mSata disk](https://es.aliexpress.com/item/33058237041.html?spm=a2g0o.order_list.order_list_main.29.1aec194duL8JLU&gatewayAdapt=glo2esp).

Debian 12 64 bits was then installed. The latest versions of golang, rust and elixir work perfectly. Although due to the limited computing capacity, they take a little while to compile.

In total, acquiring and repairing this computer cost about 15 thousand Chilean pesos (20 USD).

So if your budget is extremely tight, this can be an option to enter the beautiful world of software development and computing. It also helps the planet by reducing electronic waste.

## Option 2: Old School (8 GB of DDR3 Ram or less)

These computers can run Windows 8 to Windows 10, some versions of MacOS and Linux.

Normally for these machines we would recommend a Notebook over a desktop computer. But it depends on the budget. You can get cheap Notebooks with these characteristics that will run modern programming languages well.

Prices go up a little compared to the retro option, but you get a larger amount of computing power.

- Range: 50K to 200K CLP (60 USD - 200 USD).
- Macs from 2011 to 2017.

Examples:

- [MacMini 2011](https://support.apple.com/kb/sp632?locale=es_ES)
- [Samsung 355e](https://www.samsung.com/us/business/support/owners/product/series-3-notebook-np355e5c/)

## Option 3: Modern (16 GB of DDR4 Ram or less)

Here there is greater market diversity and prices can be really good. We recommend a desktop computer over a Notebook.

- Range: 300K to 1000K CLP (300 USD - 2000 USD).

- Macs from 2018 to 2020 (Intel Processor).

## Option 4: Ultra (32 Gb of DDR5 Ram or more)

This is the latest fashion, only for connoisseurs. If you want to secure a monster for many years and you can pay the price.

As a recommendation you can look for these specs:

- CPU: Intel i7 14700K (14th generation)
- Motherboard: Socket LGA1700 chipset Z790 ATX (Gigabyte is a good brand).
- Ram: DDR5 2x16 between 5600-6000Mhz (Corsair or Kingston is a good brand)
- Hard disk: 1 Terabyte SSD. (M.2 NVMe are still too new, wait a while before buying them). (Samsung, WD  are a good brand).
- Power supply: 800 Watt Certified
- Video card: AMD (Better compatibility with Linux)

> Thanks to PPabloTech for the specs recommendation.

Also if you go for a Mac.

- Macs from 2021 and up (with M1 processor or higher).
